<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Lycian League</h1><p class="st-meta">168 BCE – 43 CE (approx.) · capital Xanthos</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Lycian League, a federation of Lycian cities with weighted voting, praised by Strabo and cited by the framers of the US Constitution.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> freed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lycian-dynasts">Lycia</a> from Rhodian rule in 168 BCE, the Lycian cities, which had cooperated for some time, organised a formal federation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xanthos">Xanthos</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patara">Patara</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pinara">Pinara</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympos-lycia">Olympos</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/myra">Myra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlos">Tlos</a> were among its six largest members.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> describes 23 member cities sending delegates to a common assembly, with the largest cities having three votes, middling ones two and the smallest one. A federal official, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyciarch">Lyciarch</a>, presided, and the league minted common coins. Its system was later cited in the Federalist Papers.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The league kept its autonomy as a Roman ally, but after unrest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> annexed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lycian-dynasts">Lycia</a> in 43 CE, making it a province, later joined with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamphylia">Pamphylia</a>. The league survived as a provincial council.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/lycian-league.jpg" alt="Lycian League" width="320" height="214"><figcaption>Image: allen watkin from London, UK · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rock_tombs_(1091851058).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>168 BCE – 43 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Xanthos</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek and Lycian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lycian Confederacy</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-62&amp;focus=lycian-league" aria-label="Lycian League on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5431 854 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lycian League lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5751 1110L5703 1128M5751 1110L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5703 1128h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5751 1110h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5751 1110h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-62&amp;focus=lycian-league">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
